Q: How can I restrict a method to only classes implementing a specific interface in Java? A: <p>With a little changes...</p>
<pre><code>public class Healer extends GameCharacter implements Healable {

   private final int healingPower = 20;

   @Override
   public void heal( GameCharacter target ) {

        // we pass the object itself as a parameter
      target.heal( this );
   }

     // create a new method, to return the correct value 
   public int getAmount() {
      return healingPower;
   }
}

public class GameCharacter implements Mensajera {

   private String characterName;
   private int characterPower;
   private int characterMaximumHealth;
   private int characterCurrentHealth;

   // constructors, getters, etc.
   public void takeDamage( int damage ) {
      characterCurrentHealth = Math.max( 0, characterCurrentHealth - damage
      );
   }

   public void heal( Healer hel ) {

        // we obtain the correct value of the object that calls the method
      characterCurrentHealth = Math.min(
              characterMaximumHealth, characterCurrentHealth + hel.getAmount() );      
   }
}
</code></pre>

Q: how could I force some of my vue components to implement a method? A: <p>I haven't worked with Vue in a while, but while working on a personal project I remembered this question and decided to look into it again.</p>
<p>It turns out that TypeScript 4.9 introduced the <code>satisfies</code> operator after I asked this. It can be used to enforce the required methods:</p>
<pre><code>interface SellerMethods {
    updateWhenSellerChanged(): void
}

type ComponentMethods&lt;T&gt; =
    T &amp; Record&lt;string, (...args: any[]) =&gt; unknown&gt;

export default defineComponent({
    methods: {
        updateWhenSellerChanged() {
            // implementation
        },

        someOtherMethod() {
            // other methods are still allowed
        }
    } satisfies ComponentMethods&lt;SellerMethods&gt;
})
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>updateWhenSellerChanged</code> is removed, TypeScript reports that the required property is missing.</p>
<p>The <code>Record</code> part allows the component to have additional methods. If the interface contains every allowed method, this is enough:</p>
<pre><code>methods: {
    updateWhenSellerChanged() {
        // implementation
    }
} satisfies SellerMethods
</code></pre>
<p>One correction to my original example: a method should be declared as <code>updateWhenSellerChanged(): void</code>, not <code>updateWhenSellerChanged: void</code>.</p>
<p>This is still only a compile-time check. Vue components don't literally <code>implement</code> interfaces like TypeScript classes do.</p>
